 systematic imperial violence, exported it across colonies, and later retreated by destroying and hiding evidence of its policies and practices.","confidence":0.62},"academicOrientation":{"score":62,"confidence":0.58},"profileConfidence":0.74,"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"bb69ffe1a5a8318c16b31bf1ffe8548a5c5eac435fb35a782d6bd9d223e1caa5","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"History","secondarySubjects":["Politics & Government","Race & Ethnicity"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book predominantly explores the historical legacy of the British Empire, particularly focusing on its systemic use of violence in relation to governance and racial dynamics.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"db4c12cf7394888fc97a3c2ebdfcc3a802c18171b6cfa4000187c26b755de09a","summary":"From Pulitzer Prize–winning historian: a searing study of the British Empire that probes the country’s pervasive use of violence throughout the twentieth century and traces how these practices were exported, modified, and institutionalized in colonies around the globe Sprawling across a quarter of the world’s land mass and claiming nearly seven hundred million people, Britain’s twentieth-century empire was the largest empire in human history. For many Britons, it epitomized their nation’s cultural superiority, but what legacy did the island nation deliver to the world? Covering more than two hundred years of history, Caroline Elkins reveals an evolutionary and racialized doctrine that espoused an unrelenting deployment of violence to secure and preserve the nation’s imperial interests. She outlines how ideological foundations of violence were rooted in the Victorian era calls for punishing recalcitrant “natives,” and how over time, its forms became increasingly systematized. And she makes clear that when Britain could no longer maintain control over the violence it provoked and enacted, it retreated from empire, destroying and hiding incriminating evidence of its policies and practices. Drawing on more than a decade of research on four continents, Legacy of Violence implicates all sides of Britain’s political divide in the creation, execution, and cover-up of imperial violence.","publicationYear":2022,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"State-sponsored terrorism","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-legacy-of-violence-a-history-of-the-british-empire-caroline-elkins"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"History","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-legacy-of-violence-a-history-of-the-british-empire-caroline-elkins"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Punishment","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-legacy-of-violence-a-history-of-the-british-empire-caroline-elkins"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Liberalism","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-legacy-of-violence-a-history-of-the-british-empire-caroline-elkins"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Imperialism","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-legacy-of-violence-a-history-of-the-british-empire-caroline-elkins"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Colonies","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-legacy-of-violence-a-history-of-the-british-empire-caroline-elkins"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Administration","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-legacy-of-violence-a-history-of-the-british-empire-caroline-elkins"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Social conditions","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-legacy-of-violence-a-history-of-the-british-empire-caroline-elkins"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Politics and government","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-legacy-of-violence-a